    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Matador (Card Game Condition)',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "In the context of European card games, 'Matador' is not always a standalone game, but a term used to describe the most powerful cards in a game (typically trumps). Most famously used in the **Ombre** family (Ombre, Quadrille) and the **Skat** family, Matadors are cards that belong together as a sequence of high-power tokens. They often trigger specific scoring bonuses or 'payment' rules where the loser must pay the winner for every Matador held.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "The term comes from the Spanish word for 'Killer'. In the 16th-century game of Ombre, the three highest cards (Spadille, Manille, and Basto) were called Matadores.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two or more players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Spanish 40-card or German 32-card pack as per variant.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Spanish 40-card or German 32-card pack as per variant.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal according to the specific game variant (Ombre, Skat, etc.).',
              },
            ],
          },
